{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update30.json\", \"summary\": \"Validated (validate --run <run> --submission update30.json → valid=true). Closed the coverage question for the leader-lease finding by reading which lease values the retained tests actually configure. Every one keeps the lease comfortably above the leader's contact cadence: the shared in-memory configuration uses a 50 ms lease with a 50 ms heartbeat timeout and a 5 ms commit timeout, so the lease is an order of magnitude above both periodic probes (testing.go:24-34); a snapshot-heavy test raises heartbeat, election and lease timeouts together to 500 ms, preserving the ratio (raft_test.go:1440-1444); and the reload test uses a 40 ms lease with a 50 ms heartbeat timeout (2794-2797). The only lease assertion in the suite tests the intended direction — a two-node cluster loses its follower and the leader must step down within twice the lease (1780-1801) — while the configuration-validation test exercises just one invalid field, a snapshot interval below its floor (2828-2848), and leaves the lease's own interval relationships unpinned. Two honest conclusions recorded: the earlier lease finding is neither confirmed nor refuted by the suite, since no retained configuration places the lease below the cadence, so the churn case is simply never constructed; and the ten-times margin used everywhere is itself evidence of the ratio practitioners are expected to choose, which is exactly the ratio validation does not enforce. Recorded as one Fact plus an 'explained' interpretation for a new coverage question, with counterevidence preserved (the step-down test does cover the monitor's intended direction, the reload test covers value storage, and a suite that never chooses a short lease is consistent with both 'harmless in practice' and 'never considered' — the record does not adjudicate) and the explicit caveat that the cadence figures are inferred from the configured timeouts rather than measured.\"}"}}
